---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import DevFormatColor from '@/components/tools/dev/DevFormatColor.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="hex-to-hsl">
  <DevFormatColor client:load from="hex" to="hsl" />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>What HSL measures</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Hue is a position on the colour wheel in degrees: 0 red, 60 yellow, 120 green, 180 cyan, 240 blue, 300 magenta, then back to 360.</li>
      <li>Saturation is the distance of the colour from grey: 0% is grey and 100% is fully vivid at that lightness.</li>
      <li>Lightness runs from 0% (black) to 100% (white), and 50% is the pure hue. This axis makes HSL useful, and people often confuse it with brightness.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>The conversion</h2>
    <pre><code>#2563eb → r=37 g=99 b=235, normalised to 0–1
  max = 0.922 (blue)   min = 0.145 (red)   delta = 0.777

  L = (max + min) / 2                = 0.533 → 53.3%
  S = delta / (1 - |2L - 1|)         = 0.833 → 83.3%
  H = 60 × (4 + (r - g) / delta)     = 220.9°

  hsl(220.9, 83.3%, 53.3%)</code></pre>
    <p>
      The hue formula has three branches, one for each channel that can be the maximum. The one shown is
      the blue-max case. The page computes all values in full precision and rounds only for display, so
      hex → HSL → hex returns the original byte.
    </p>

    <h2>Building a tint and shade scale</h2>
    <p>
      This is the main reason to convert HEX to HSL. Keep the hue and saturation and change lightness in
      even steps. You get a coherent scale in one pass, without guessing hex codes.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>HSL</th><th>Typical use</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>50</td><td>hsl(221, 83%, 96%)</td><td>Tinted background</td></tr>
        <tr><td>100</td><td>hsl(221, 83%, 91%)</td><td>Hover on a tinted background</td></tr>
        <tr><td>300</td><td>hsl(221, 83%, 74%)</td><td>Disabled state, borders</td></tr>
        <tr><td>500</td><td>hsl(221, 83%, 53%)</td><td>The base colour</td></tr>
        <tr><td>700</td><td>hsl(221, 83%, 40%)</td><td>Hover on a solid button</td></tr>
        <tr><td>900</td><td>hsl(221, 83%, 24%)</td><td>Text on a tinted background</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      Reduce saturation slightly at both ends. At very high lightness a fully saturated hue looks chalky,
      and at very low lightness it looks muddy. With saturation 10–20 points lower at the ends, the scale
      looks like one family.
    </p>

    <h2>Harmony from the hue number</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>For a complementary colour, add 180 to the hue.</li>
      <li>For analogous colours, use ±20 to ±40, for a low-contrast, related palette.</li>
      <li>For a triadic palette, use ±120.</li>
      <li>For split complementary, use 180 ± 30. This is usually easier to use than a straight complement.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>HSL is not perceptually uniform</h2>
    <p>
      Two colours with the same HSL lightness can look very different in brightness:
      <code>hsl(60, 100%, 50%)</code> (yellow) is visibly lighter than <code>hsl(240, 100%, 50%)</code>
      (blue), because human vision is much more sensitive to green than to blue. So a scale that changes
      only L does not have even perceived steps across different hues, and equal L does not mean equal
      contrast. When that matters, use Oklch. HSL is still practical for one hue family.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
